{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>A man's vessel shall not be upon a woman.</b> Scripture forbade weapons to a woman lest she go out to war and this become a cause for licentiousness; and likewise a man, if he wears a woman's garment and mingles among women — all this is an abomination of YHWH. And so our Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted: ״כי תועבת ה' אלהיך״ — a matter that leads to abomination. And from this verse we learn, our Rabbis of blessed memory from oral tradition, that it is forbidden for a person to adorn himself with the adornment particular to them, such as looking in a mirror and the like; and this is one of the three things that the Sages needed to permit for the house of Rabbi. Hear from this that it is forbidden for the rest of people to look in a mirror, because it is an adornment of women — unless he is a scribe, whom they permitted to look in order to strengthen the power of sight; or if he is ill and frail and wishes to look at the appearance of his face — this is permitted, for this is not included under adornment and is not a way of desire and the enticements of the evil inclination. And furthermore our Rabbis of blessed memory said: one who picks among black chickpeas is liable under לא ילבש גבר שמלת אשה. And furthermore our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted that the reason Yael killed Sisera with a tent peg and did not kill him with a knife is because of לא יהיה כלי גבר על אשה."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:50:08.435073720Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}